# Copy to stu.env (gitignored) and fill in. start.sh sources this; every value can also be
# passed as a flag, and a flag wins. Nothing here is a secret — the key is a separate file — but
# every value names a specific client's data, which is why none of them are committed.

# Project slug. Lowercase, alphanumeric and hyphens. The BigQuery dataset is derived from it:
# slug "acme" reads research_acme.
STU_SLUG=acme

# Google Cloud project holding that dataset.
# Console → project picker → the project ID, not the display name.
STU_PROJECT=acme-research-123456

# UUID of the Buzz channel this research lives in. Buzz installs only, and optional even there —
# it records which channel an identity came from. Leave it blank off Buzz.
STU_CHANNEL=

# Who is editing. Set this and the app shows you for confirmation at launch instead of asking.
#
# STU_USER_EMAIL is enough on its own, and is the normal answer outside Buzz — an email address
# is a perfectly good identity to record an edit against. On Buzz, prefer the pubkey: it is the
# same identity the channel uses and it survives an email change. Setting both is fine; the
# pubkey wins as the identity and the email is stored alongside it.
#
# A pubkey is 64 hex characters, not an npub: Buzz Desktop -> your profile.
STU_USER_EMAIL=
STU_USER_NAME=
STU_USER_PUBKEY=

# Loopback port. Change it to run two channels side by side.
STU_PORT=4317

# Path to the BigQuery service-account key. Leave unset to use the search order in start.sh:
# ./secrets/claire-<slug>-service-user.json, then ~/.buzz/.secrets/claire-<slug>-service-user.json.
#STU_BQ_KEY=
